    "abstract": "The rapid growth of online food delivery services has transformed how consumers access meals in urban areas, offering the convenience of doorstep delivery. This growing demand has intensified pressure on platform operators to prioritise speed and efficiency, often using short delivery times as a key performance metric. As a result, delivery riders are subjected to time constraints in congested traffic conditions, which can compromise their safety. This study investigates whether consumers, as the end beneficiaries of such services, are willing to support safer delivery practices by making trade-offs in terms of cost and waiting time. A stated preference experiment on 350 regular users of the online food delivery system in Amsterdam and Copenhagen examined how safety labels indicating rider crash risk influence their behaviour. A hybrid latent class choice model was employed to capture preference heterogeneity and incorporate attitudinal factors. The analysis revealed two distinct consumer segments: one generally indifferent to road safety and another highly sensitive about it. Results suggest that while some consumers remain unaffected, a sizable segment is influenced by safety labels and exhibits a clear willingness to pay more or wait longer for safer deliveries. The study presents several managerial and policy insights, outlining practical strategies that could encourage consumers to make choices that help improve the safety of food delivery riders.’",
