  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely on stated preference survey data from two specific European cities, which may reflect higher baseline safety awareness than the actual behavior of the broader consumer population.",
      "what": "A stated preference experiment surveyed 350 regular food delivery users in Amsterdam and Copenhagen to evaluate how safety labels indicating rider crash risk affect consumer choices. Using a hybrid latent class choice model, the study analyzed consumer willingness to accept longer wait times or higher costs in exchange for safer working conditions for riders.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that a distinct segment of urban consumers is willing to trade personal convenience and money for rider safety when presented with explicit risk labels, offering a non-regulatory pathway to mitigate the traffic hazards faced by gig workers."
    }
